You are authoring the **implementation document** for a single leaf in
a UI collection's component tree — the last articulation layer before
code territory. Your downstream reader is the **plan prompt**, which
translates your prose into a concrete list of (file, region, change)
tuples. Same grammar as the backend chain's own `impl` tier — this
tier's distinctive constraint is that everything you describe is
**stateless rendering**: no fetch, no backend call, no subscription. If
you find yourself describing one, that behavior belongs to a screen
collection's own leaf, not this one.

You will be given the owning leaf's metadata (name, techspec, public
and private surface) and, optionally, a prior draft, user feedback, and
a parse-validate error. You produce a single `<implementation>` block
containing six sections in a fixed order: behavior, invariants,
sequencing, edge-cases, tests, and references (optional).

# Input

Leaf (its own techspec, public and private surface): {{ parent }}

Attached references:
{% for ref in reference %}
### {{ ref.title }}

{{ ref.body }}
{% endfor %}

# Output format

Emit exactly one `<implementation>` block with these children in this
order: `<behavior>` → `<invariants>` → `<sequencing>` →
`<edge-cases>` → `<tests>` → `<references>` (optional). Example
(abbreviated):

    <implementation>
      <behavior>
    Renders an `<img>` tag when `image_url` is present; falls back to
    a div showing `initials` otherwise. The presence ring is a CSS
    class computed from the `presence` prop and applied to the
    wrapping element — no runtime style computation, no JS.
      </behavior>
      <invariants>
    The rendered markup never includes a broken-image reference: a nil
    or empty `image_url` always renders the initials branch, never an
    `<img src="">`. The presence ring class is a pure function of the
    `presence` prop — the same prop value always produces the same
    class, with no memoized or stale state carried between renders.
      </invariants>
      <sequencing>
    Pure render, no ordering concerns — there is no async work, no
    mount lifecycle, no event handling in this leaf.
      </sequencing>
      <edge-cases>
    A `presence` value outside the known enum renders with no ring
    class rather than raising — a stale client is expected to send one
    occasionally, and failing render entirely would be worse than a
    missing ring. `initials` longer than two characters is truncated
    at render time, not upstream, so the parent doesn't need to know
    this leaf's own display rule.
      </edge-cases>
      <tests>
        <test name="renders_image_when_url_present">A non-nil
        image_url renders an img tag with that src.</test>
        <test name="falls_back_to_initials_when_url_absent">A nil
        image_url renders the initials div, never a broken img.</test>
        <test name="unknown_presence_renders_without_ring">A presence
        value outside the known enum renders with no ring class rather
        than raising.</test>
      </tests>
    </implementation>

# Rules

## Structure

* Emit **exactly one** `<implementation>` root block. The children
  **must appear in this order**: `<behavior>` → `<invariants>` →
  `<sequencing>` → `<edge-cases>` → `<tests>` → `<references>` (omit
  entirely when nothing is attached).

## Content

* `<behavior>`, `<invariants>`, `<sequencing>`, `<edge-cases>` are
  non-empty prose, the same discipline the backend `impl` tier uses.
  `<sequencing>` is frequently short or nearly empty at this tier — a
  pure render has little ordering to describe; say so plainly rather
  than padding it.
* **Nothing here issues a network call, reads a socket, or holds
  server-round-trip state.** Every prop this leaf receives came from
  its caller already resolved; describe what the leaf does with what
  it's given, never how the data got there.

## Tests

* `<tests>` holds one or more `<test name="...">` entries, covering
  the primary render paths named in `<behavior>` and every distinct
  condition `<edge-cases>` names, at minimum.

## References

* `<references>` is optional — omit entirely when nothing is attached.

## Style

* Prose, not pseudocode or markup. Don't restate the leaf's own public
  surface — the plan prompt reads that separately.
* Keep it proportionate: a simple render leaf doesn't need the same
  length as a stateful backend leaf with real invariants to hold.

## Meta-rules

* Unescaped `&` and `<` in prose are fine — the parser tolerates them.
